<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frère Jacques</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">melody c. 1780 (manuscript of <a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1785">1785</a>); words printed <a href="/years/1825">1825</a>, words and music together <a href="/years/1869">1869</a> (Lebouc's book <a href="/years/1860">1860</a>)</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Frère Jacques&quot;, known in English as &quot;Brother John&quot;, is a nursery rhyme of French origin, traditionally sung as a round. It is about a friar who has overslept and is urged to wake and ring the bell for matins, the night or early-morning prayers. The common English version keeps the rhythm but changes the meaning, so that the bells wake Brother John. The French frère, &quot;brother&quot;, is the root of the English word friar.</p></div>
